# engineering-workflow

## Overview

Define the outcome, plan substantial changes, implement, verify, review, and report. Existing user authorization carries forward.

## When to Use

Implementation, debugging, reviews, and release preparation. Use targeted checks for routine maintenance. A direct question needs an answer; skip an implementation lifecycle unless a change is requested.

## Rules & Patterns

Establish acceptance criteria for substantial or ambiguous work. Ask only for missing decisions affecting scope, safety, or external actions. An implementation request authorizes ordinary reversible work. Inspect affected code and callers, preserve unrelated changes, and verify behavior before reporting completion. Roles are optional.

Match the requested outcome: a question, review, or plan does not itself authorize implementation. For an implementation request, a plan or suggested patch alone is not completion; perform the available authorized work and relevant checks. Stop when the acceptance criteria are met or a concrete blocker needs unavailable access, a user decision, or an action outside existing authority. Complete independent in-scope work before handing off a blocker; do not bypass safety boundaries or retry indefinitely.

Read [references/workflow.md](../../.agents/core/skills/engineering-workflow/references/workflow.md) for procedures when needed.

## Code Examples

A README typo needs a small edit and formatting check. An authorization fix needs an allowed-user case and a denied-user regression.

## Validation Checklist

- [ ] Changed behavior and affected callers satisfy the request; failures have a concrete reproduction when feasible.
- [ ] Checks match the change's risk. An unavailable check is reported with the remaining uncertainty.
- [ ] The diff preserves unrelated work; delivery stays within existing authorization.

## Common Mistakes

Repeated approval after authorization; full ceremonies for routine edits; treating headings, role labels, or schema checks as behavioral proof.

## Integration Notes

Use security for sensitive boundaries, ponytail-mindset for implementation complexity, and context-os for compiler/configuration work. gstack-roles is a compatibility alias.
